Kubernetes Deployment Strategies — From Scratch to Production 🚀

If you’re learning Kubernetes or working as a DevOps Engineer, understanding deployment strategies is a must. Here’s a simple, end-to-end view 👇

🔹 1. Recreate Strategy

Old pods are terminated first

New pods are created afterward
 Downtime expected

 Simple & useful for non-critical apps

🔹 2. Rolling Update (Default in K8s)

Pods are updated gradually

Zero or minimal downtime

Controlled using maxSurge & maxUnavailable

 Best for most production workloads

🔹 3. Blue-Green Deployment

Two environments: Blue (current) & Green (new)

Switch traffic instantly

Easy rollback

 Ideal for critical applications

🔹 4. Canary Deployment

Release to a small % of users first

Monitor metrics & logs

Gradually increase traffic

 Reduces production risk

🔹 5. A/B Testing

Multiple versions run simultaneously

Traffic split based on rules

Used for feature validation & experiments

🔹 6. Shadow Deployment

New version receives real traffic (without affecting users)

Used for performance & behavior testing

🔹 From Scratch to End – Real Flow
 Build Image → Push to Registry
 Create Deployment & Service
 Choose Strategy (Rolling / Blue-Green / Canary)
 Monitor (Logs, Metrics, Alerts)
 Rollback if needed

💡 Tip: Combine Kubernetes with Ingress, Service Mesh (Istio), CI/CD tools (Jenkins/GitHub Actions/GitLab) for advanced traffic control.
